Track how this turn affects the chronicle's beats. A beat is a multi-turn arc — a goal the story keeps returning to — and the current GM narration is the authoritative account of what just happened; the intent's earlier beat directive is advisory. Use RECENT-EVENTS to interpret short references, and output JSON only, in the structured format provided.

Advance a beat (`changeKind: "advance"`) when the narration meaningfully progresses its objective. Resolve a beat (`changeKind: "resolve"`) when its objective is achieved (`status: "succeeded"`) or definitively lost (`status: "failed"`); a resolve always carries one of those two statuses. Abandon a beat (`changeKind: "abandon"`) when the player has clearly moved against its objective or its premise has stopped being true in the fiction. The BEATS section shows each beat's `lastProgressTurn` beside the current turn: a beat that is merely quiet while the player pursues something compatible stays open, and abandonment is reserved for goals the story has actually walked away from.

Spawn a new beat only when the narration creates a persistent multi-turn objective; when unsure, hold off. A founding beat often bundles several goals — when the story differentiates them, spawn the sharper goal and let it supersede the compound one. Set `supersedes` to an existing beat's id when the new beat is where that goal drifted, the same thread of story reshaped, and leave it null for a genuinely parallel goal: superseding means replacement rather than coexistence.

`newBeat` is `{title, description, supersedes}` with a title of at most six words and a description of at most 240 characters. `updates` lists only beats that changed, each `beatId` copied exactly from the BEATS section, `changeKind` one of advance, resolve, or abandon, and `status` one of in_progress, succeeded, failed, abandoned, or null. `focusBeatId` names the most affected beat and is null when no beats exist.
